# Code quality

Inspired by pragmatic Clean Architecture in React ([Kondov / Full-Stack Tao](https://alexkondov.com/full-stack-tao-clean-architecture-react/)) plus SOLID. Details: `docs/architecture-plan.md` §4 and ADR-0004.

## SOLID (web)

- **S — Single responsibility:** Components render. Hooks/use-cases orchestrate. `lib/api` transports. `lib/display` maps DTO → view models. No dayjs/entitlement math in JSX.
- **O — Open/closed:** Extend via new mappers/modules; avoid editing core HTTP client for every screen.
- **L — Liskov:** Port implementations (real API, fake for tests) must honor the same contract/types.
- **I — Interface segregation:** Small ports (`RenewalsReader`, `OtpAuth`) — not one god `Api` used everywhere in UI.
- **D — Dependency inversion:** UI and use-cases depend on **ports/interfaces**, not `fetch`/axios concretes. Wire concretes at the composition root (route/server entry, factory).

## Dependency injection

- Prefer **constructor/factory injection** (pass deps into use-case factories or hooks as args).
- Avoid hidden globals for API clients in features; one composition place builds the graph.
- Test by injecting fakes that implement the same port — do not mock JSX internals.

## Clean Architecture habits (Kondov)

- Design for the **domain / access pattern** first; ask questions before assuming API shapes.
- First draft may be messy; **extract layers before shipping** (HTTP out of components, then display mapping out of JSX).
- Prefer **deep modules** (small public surface, rich internals) over shallow wrappers.
- Extract endpoint URLs / query keys next to the transport layer — no scattered magic strings.
- Validate API payloads at the transport boundary (schema) — do not cast and pray.
- Layers change at different rates: JSX often; HTTP rarely — keep them separate.

## Baseline hygiene

- TypeScript strict; no `any`; no messy barrel dumps.
- Named handlers and typed props; avoid anonymous sprawl of state.
- Glossary names from `docs/CONTEXT.md` (GymOrg, DataGrant, Subscription line, Lead).
- Small, focused modules; no premature frameworks — pragmatism over ceremony.
- Performance: lazy routes where natural; avoid unnecessary client JS.
- Errors: calm user-facing messages; never expose stack traces or internals in UI.
